# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: ci-job-activity-cap-refuses-master-pipelines — a merge can land on master with NO pipeline at all, and nothing says so
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-ci-job-activity-cap
#+DATE: 2026-09-27

* Observed

Two consecutive master pipelines, 2026-09-26 21:47Z and 21:55Z, are
=failed= with *zero jobs*, zero duration, and =yaml_errors: null=. The REST
pipeline endpoint shows nothing but the failure; GraphQL names it:

#+begin_example
query { project(fullPath:"ogamita/clautolisp") { pipeline(iid:"2484") {
  status failureReason jobs { count } } } }

  status        = FAILED
  failureReason = "The pipeline job activity limit was exceeded."
  jobs.count    = 0
#+end_example

| pipeline    | iid  | sha      | created              |
|-------------+------+----------+----------------------|
| 2885863084  | 2483 | 12bb8299 | 2026-09-26T21:55:04Z |
| 2885863269  | 2484 | 7d56d37b | 2026-09-26T21:55:12Z |

Both are merge commits on =master=. *Neither ran any CI.* The merges are in
the history with no verification behind them, and the only trace is a red
pipeline that looks exactly like an ordinary failure — which is how it was
read for the two hours before someone asked GraphQL.

* Why this is not the closed ticket

=issues/closed/ci-native-child-pipeline-creation-fails.issue= diagnosed the
same COUNTER filling up, and its durable fix #1 is
=native:pipeline: allow_failure: true= — so that a BRIDGE which cannot
create its child fails softly instead of reddening the parent.

That fix cannot help here, and the difference matters: *it is the PARENT
pipeline that is refused now*, at creation, before any job exists. There is
no bridge to allow-fail and no job to skip. A pipeline that was never
created cannot report its own absence, so the failure is silent by
construction: CI's own gates are exactly what is missing.

* Cause — measured, not inferred

163 jobs were in the states that count toward the cap (=pending= 124,
=created= 30, =running= 9). Where they were:

| pipeline    | ref                          | source          | active jobs |
|-------------+------------------------------+-----------------+-------------|
| 2886027994  | master                       | parent_pipeline |          38 |
| 2886027801  | master                       | push            |          25 |
| 2885989591  | refs/merge-requests/297/head | parent_pipeline |          21 |
| 2885864174  | refs/merge-requests/295/head | parent_pipeline |          21 |
| 2885851115  | refs/merge-requests/294/head | parent_pipeline |          21 |
| 2885836781  | refs/merge-requests/293/head | parent_pipeline |          21 |
| 2782105481  | master                       | push            |          15 |
| 2885828771  | refs/merge-requests/292/head | parent_pipeline |           1 |

Three distinct defects in the existing broom, each independently sufficient:

** 1. The sweep's criterion is AGE, and this leak is minutes old

=scripts/cancel-stale-child-pipelines.py= cancels active children older
than =SWEEP_MAX_AGE_HOURS= (12). The four children above belong to merge
requests !293, !294, !295 and !297 — *all four state=merged* — and all four
were under two hours old. They hold 84 active jobs between them and the
sweep would not have touched one of them, at any hour of the night.

Their manual jobs can never be played: the merge request they belong to is
gone. Age is the wrong question. *Whether anyone could ever play them* is
the right one, and it is answerable — the ref is
=refs/merge-requests/N/head= and the MR's state is one API call away.

This is the gap the script's own docstring names ("a merge request merged
from its last push, which is the normal case — has nothing to supersede it,
and its child lives for ever") and then does not close: it drains those by
age, once a night, which is far too slow for a repository whose working
pattern is an evening of agent-merged MRs.

** 2. It runs once a night, so the bucket empties at 03:00Z

The cap was crossed at 21:47Z. The next sweep was due at 03:00Z — five
hours during which every master pipeline would have been refused. The job
takes *two seconds* (03:08:04 → 03:08:06 in its own trace) on a docker
runner. There is no reason for it to be nightly-only.

** 3. Its listing is capped at 500 of 806 children, and it says so in a line nobody read

From the 2026-09-26 03:08Z run, verbatim:

#+begin_example
child pipelines seen: 500, active: 0, older than 12h: 0
nothing to sweep; the 0 active child pipeline(s) are all younger than 12h
#+end_example

=seen: 500= is exactly =for page in range(1, 6)= x 100 — the page cap, not
a count. Paging to exhaustion finds *806*. So the sweep is blind to every
child older than the newest 500, which in this repository is a few weeks:
an immortal child that drifts past the window is never swept again, and the
sweep prints a reassuring "nothing to sweep" in precisely the situation it
exists to catch. Same class as the release check that passed a 42 MB
archive no launcher could open.

** And a fourth, outside the sweep's scope entirely: a stuck PARENT

Pipeline 2782105481, =master=, =source: push=, created *2026-08-22T17:55Z*,
is still =running= five weeks later. Its jobs: 15 =success=, 1 =manual=,
and *15 in =created== — =pages=, =deploy:documentation=, the five
=benchmark:*= and the seven =release:*= jobs — waiting behind a stage that
never completed (=updated_at= is 2026-08-22T18:01:38Z).

Those 15 jobs have counted against the cap for five weeks, and nothing
sweeps them: the script lists =source=parent_pipeline= only, by design and
by name. Roughly one tenth of the ceiling, held permanently, by one
forgotten pipeline.

* Fix direction

1. *Add a state criterion to the sweep, independent of age.* An active
   child whose ref is =refs/merge-requests/N/head= and whose MR is =merged=
   or =closed= is stale IMMEDIATELY. Keep the age rule for everything else
   (a branch pushed once and abandoned still needs it).
2. *Run the sweep on every master pipeline*, in =.pre=, as well as
   nightly. It costs two seconds and it is idempotent. It must never cancel
   the pipeline it is running in, nor that pipeline's own child.
3. *Page to exhaustion, and never report success from a truncated
   listing.* If the listing stops at a page cap, that is a failure to
   report, not a number to print. Better still, ask for the active states
   directly — active children are few, so the window stops mattering.
4. *Cover stuck parents* under a separate, much larger threshold (a
   legitimately long master pipeline waiting on a serial Windows queue
   takes hours; five weeks is not that). The script then sweeps stale
   PIPELINES, not only children, and should be named accordingly.
5. *Make the log tell the truth*: print the active-job total and the
   headroom, so a filling bucket is visible in the one place that looks at
   it every night, before it refuses a merge.

* Acceptance criteria

- A child pipeline of a merged or closed MR is cancelled by the sweep
  regardless of age, and a test proves the selection (not just that the
  script runs).
- The sweep runs automatically on master pipelines, and provably never
  cancels its own pipeline or its own child.
- A truncated pipeline listing is a FAILURE, not a printed number; the
  count in the log is a real count.
- A stuck parent pipeline older than the parent threshold is cancelled.
- The log reports active jobs and headroom.
- =make= target + CI lane, as =check-nightly-sweep-isolation= already does
  for the other half of this mechanism.

* Not done here, and why

The bucket was still full when this was filed. Cancelling the four
merged-MR children by hand was refused by this session's tool policy
(cancelling live CI workloads), which is a reasonable guard rather than an
obstacle: the project has its own sanctioned mechanism for exactly this,
=sweep:stale-child-pipelines=, and the right move is to make that mechanism
correct and then play it. So the draining happens through the fixed sweep,
not through an agent cancelling pipelines by hand.

* Implemented — clautolisp 2.2.126

=scripts/cancel-stale-child-pipelines.py= is replaced by
=scripts/cancel-stale-pipelines.py= (renamed: it no longer sweeps only
children, and a name that lies about its scope is how the stuck parent stayed
invisible). The job is =sweep:stale-pipelines=.

1. *State criterion, independent of age.* =classify_pipeline= sweeps a child
   whose ref is =refs/merge-requests/N/head= when that MR is =merged= or
   =closed=, whatever its age: nobody can play its manual jobs again. An
   *open* MR's child of the same age is left alone — that is the half that
   matters, since sweeping it would kill the verification of every merge
   request under review. An MR state that could not be read falls back to
   age, so an API hiccup never becomes a cancellation.
2. *Runs on every master pipeline* (=.pre=, =allow_failure: true=) as well as
   nightly and by hand. Two seconds, idempotent.
3. *Listing is exhaustive, and truncation is a FAILURE.* The query asks per
   ACTIVE status, so the sets are small; if a listing would still be
   truncated, =active_pipelines= raises and the run reports FAIL instead of
   printing a page cap as if it were a count.
4. *Parents too*, on age alone under a separate generous threshold
   (=SWEEP_MAX_PARENT_AGE_HOURS=, 48) — a master pipeline waiting on the
   serial Windows runner legitimately stays open for hours.
5. *Never its own pipeline, nor its own child*: =protected_ids= reads
   =CI_PIPELINE_ID= and its bridges. Not hypothetical now that the sweep runs
   in =.pre= of every master pipeline — without it the job would cancel the
   pipeline it belongs to on its first run.
6. *The log says how full the bucket is*: =count_active_jobs= prints the jobs
   counting against the cap. Not the headroom — GitLab does not expose the
   limit over the API — but the number that moves, in the one place anybody
   looks at this mechanism.

=scripts/tests/test-cancel-stale-pipelines.py= (17 tests, =make
check-stale-pipeline-sweep=, wired into the CI documentation lane): every case
is one the previous sweep got wrong, in pairs so the check can fail — the
merged-MR child IS swept and the open-MR child of the same age is NOT.

*The tests were watched failing against the old rule* rather than assumed to
discriminate: with the MR-state and parent clauses removed, a 0.5 h child of a
merged MR and the five-week-old parent both classify as =None= — the 84 jobs
and the 15 jobs that filled the cap, exactly.

*API assumptions verified against the live project*, not read from the docs:
=status== filters, the plain listing EXCLUDES children (3 results, none with
=source=parent_pipeline=, while the child listing returned 6), and =source= is
present on every entry — so classification does not depend on which listing an
entry came from.

** Observed on master, and the selection is right — but the token cannot cancel

=sweep:stale-pipelines= ran in =.pre= of master pipeline 2886065813 (job
16758514768, clautolisp 2.2.126). The selection is exactly right:

#+begin_example
active pipelines: 9 (1 protected), to cancel: 8
jobs counting against the activity cap: unknown
  FAILED 2782105481 master           pipeline still active after 846.2h (>= 48h) -- stuck: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2885836781 !293/head        child of !293, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2885851115 !294/head        child of !294, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2885864174 !295/head        child of !295, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2885989591 !297/head        child of !297, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2886033287 !298/head        child of !298, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2886048719 !299/head        child of !299, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
  FAILED 2886059142 !300/head        child of !300, which is merged ...: HTTP Error 403: Forbidden
FAIL: 8 cancellation(s) refused. If the token has read_api but not api,
      this is exactly what it looks like -- widen the scope.
#+end_example

Eight pipelines, each with its reason: the five-week-old stuck parent (846
hours) and the children of *seven* merged merge requests. The previous sweep
would have selected NONE of them. Its own protected-id rule kept it off the
pipeline it was running in. So criteria 1, 4, 5 and the log are confirmed
against the live project, not only in tests.

*And then every cancellation was refused with HTTP 403.* The diagnostic that
fired was written by the previous author for exactly this, and it is right:
=RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN= (a *group-level* masked variable on =ogamita=, not a
project one) can READ — the listing worked — and cannot WRITE. Reading runner
status needs only =read_api=; cancelling a pipeline needs =api=.

*Which means the broom has never swept anything, for two independent reasons.*
The age-only rule inside the newest-500 window found nothing to cancel, so the
403 that would have met it was never reached, so the nightly job printed
"nothing to sweep" and looked healthy for weeks. Fixing the selection is what
made the second defect visible — and neither would have surfaced without
watching a run.

** What remains before this closes — a token, which is pjb's to grant

The sweep needs a credential with the =api= scope. Two shapes, and the second
is better hygiene:

1. widen =RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN= (group =ogamita=) to =api=; or
2. add a SECOND group variable, e.g. =PIPELINE_SWEEP_TOKEN=, with =api=, and
   leave =RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN= read-only. =detect:runners= only reads, and a
   read-only token should stay read-only — the sweep is the one job that needs
   to write, and it can name its own credential.

Not done here deliberately: creating a project/group access token and setting a
masked CI variable is a credential change on pjb's namespace, not a background
action. Cancelling the eight pipelines by hand was also refused by this
session's tool policy, which is the same judgement from the other side.

Until the token exists the sweep runs, reports precisely what it WOULD cancel,
and fails =allow_failure= — so it is visible on every master pipeline without
blocking anything. That is strictly better than the silence it replaces, but it
is not yet a drain.

* Note

Found while reading why two master pipelines were red before playing the
Windows AutoCAD evidence jobs. Filed per AGENTS.md rather than mentioned:
a merge that lands with no CI is the most expensive kind of silence this
repository can have.
